<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob Kettler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1682">1682</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Duke Jacob of Courland, Jacob, Duke of Courland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob Kettler was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia from 1642 to 1682, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Influenced by mercantilist ideas, he built ironworks, shipyards and a large merchant fleet, and founded colonies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> River and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, making Courland one of the smallest states with overseas possessions. The Swedish invasion of 1658 ruined much of his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler.jpg" alt="Jacob Kettler" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacob_Kettler.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
